[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that are congruent to 1 modulo 10. Since a number ending in 1 modulo 10 must be coprime to 10, we consider the divisors of \(9!\) that are coprime to 10. These divisors are of the form \(3^b \times 7^d\) where \(0 \leq b \leq 4\) and \(0 \leq d \leq 1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m Listing these divisors, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m - For \(d = 0\): \(1, 3, 9, 27, 81\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m - For \(d = 1\): \(7, 21, 63, 189, 567\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m We then check which of these divisors are congruent to 1 modulo 10:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m - \(1 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m - \(21 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m - \(81 \equiv 1 \mod 10\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=97055)[0m Thus, the divisors are \(1, 21, 81\). The sum of these divisors is:
